Id: no-uses-without-a-pinned-version
Pattern: ^[[:space:]]*(-[[:space:]]+)?uses:[[:space:]]+([^./[:space:]][^@[:space:]]*([[:space:]]|$)|[^[:space:]]+@([0-9a-f]*[^0-9a-f[:space:]]|[0-9a-f]{1,39}([[:space:]]|$)|[0-9a-f]{41,}|[0-9a-f]{40}[[:space:]]*$))
Subject: .github/workflows
Except: none
Issue: 27

A gate is worth what it can say about itself afterwards. A step named by a
branch or a tag ran whatever those bytes were at the moment the run started,
and nothing in this repository records what that was, so a green run a month
ago cannot be read as a statement about anything. That is #27's complaint and
this is the half of it that a machine can hold.

The rule refuses four spellings, and they are one decision reached four ways.

A ref that is not hexadecimal is a name somebody else can move: a branch always,
and a tag whenever the owner chooses to. A `uses:` with no ref at all is the
same thing spelled by omission, because the default branch answers.

A hexadecimal ref that is not forty characters is an abbreviation. Git accepts
one against the repository in front of it and the forge resolves it against a
repository that keeps growing, so the bytes an abbreviation names are a function
of when it was resolved rather than of what was written.

A forty-character pin with no trailing comment is the case this rule exists for
that no other check here holds. The pin is correct and unreadable: nothing on
the line, and nothing anywhere else in the tree, says which release those bytes
are. A reader deciding whether an advisory applies has to resolve the sha
against the network to find out, which is the work the comment exists to save,
and a renovate bump that drops the comment leaves the pin looking fine.

A path beginning with `./` is excepted by the pattern rather than by the
`Except` field, because a call into this repository's own workflows is already
at this commit and there is nothing to pin it to. That is the only legitimate
`uses:` without a sha, and writing it into the pattern keeps the exception next
to the reason for it.

Nothing else is excepted. The subject is the workflow directory, so the rule
reaches every workflow this repository owns and every wrapper it has kept, and
it does not reach the prose that describes it.

What it cannot do. It reads a line at a time, so it judges the shape of a
comment and never its truth: `# v1.2.3` against a sha from a different release
passes, and so does `# see the pull request`. Nothing here checks that the sha
exists, that it belongs to the repository named beside it, or that the tag the
comment names ever pointed at it. Renovate is what keeps those honest today and
this rule is what notices when the comment stops being written at all.

It also says nothing about what a pinned action does. A sha is a statement that
the bytes did not move, not that they were ever safe to run, and the audit that
judges the workflows themselves is a different check with a different subject.
